en: '<div class="math-prereq" style="background:rgba(245,158,11,0.07);border-left:3px solid #f59e0b;padding:0.95rem 1.2rem;margin:0 0 1.5rem 0;border-radius:0 8px 8px 0"><div style="font-size:0.74rem;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:0.1em;color:#f59e0b;margin-bottom:0.5rem">📐 MATH FOUNDATIONS</div><p style="margin:0 0 0.55rem 0;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.55;color:rgba(235,230,220,0.85)">New to the math used here? Refresh these first — each is a self-contained Mathematics lesson:</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:1.25rem;font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.7;color:rgba(235,230,220,0.85);list-style:none"><li><a href="/tutorials/matematik/40" style="color:#f59e0b;text-decoration:none;border-bottom:1px dotted #f59e0b">Functions &amp; Notation</a> <span style="opacity:0.55;font-size:0.82em">(Math L40)</span></li><li><a href="/tutorials/matematik/72" style="color:#f59e0b;text-decoration:none;border-bottom:1px dotted #f59e0b">Matrices &amp; Operations</a> <span style="opacity:0.55;font-size:0.82em">(Math L72)</span></li><li><a href="/tutorials/matematik/91" style="color:#f59e0b;text-decoration:none;border-bottom:1px dotted #f59e0b">Geometric Vectors</a> <span style="opacity:0.55;font-size:0.82em">(Math L91)</span></li></ul></div><p class="l-text"><strong>NumPy is the bedrock of scientific computing in Python.</strong> Every machine learning library you will ever use -- scikit-learn, TensorFlow, PyTorch, Pandas -- is built on top of NumPy arrays. Understanding how to create, inspect, and manipulate these arrays is the single most important skill for any data scientist or ML engineer.</p>'

+ '<p class="l-text">This lesson takes you from zero to confident: why NumPy exists, how its core object (the <strong>ndarray</strong>) works, every creation function you will need, data types, reshaping, and real-world patterns you will use daily.</p>'
+ '<div class="lesson-outcomes" style="background:rgba(200,169,110,0.06);border-left:3px solid #c8a96e;padding:1rem 1.2rem;margin:1.5rem 0;border-radius:0 8px 8px 0"><div style="font-size:0.78rem;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:0.08em;color:#c8a96e;margin-bottom:0.6rem">📍 WHAT YOU\'LL LEARN</div><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:1.3rem;line-height:1.65;font-size:0.93rem;color:rgba(235,230,220,0.92)"><li>Explain why NumPy is 10–100x faster than Python lists for numeric work</li><li>Create ndarrays with zeros, ones, full, eye, arange, linspace, and logspace</li><li>Inspect array attributes: shape, dtype, ndim, size, itemsize, and nbytes</li><li>Pick the right dtype to balance precision and memory footprint</li><li>Reshape arrays and reason about 1D vectors, 2D matrices, and 3D+ tensors</li></ul></div>'


/* ============================================================
   SECTION 1: Why NumPy?
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">1. Why NumPy?</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>The short answer:</strong> Python lists are slow. NumPy arrays are fast. For numerical work, NumPy is <strong>10x to 100x faster</strong> than pure Python because it delegates computation to optimized C/Fortran code and operates on contiguous blocks of memory.</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text">Consider adding two lists of 1 million numbers. In pure Python, you need a for-loop that iterates one million times, calling Python\'s interpreter at each step. NumPy replaces that entire loop with a <strong>single C-level operation</strong> called <strong>vectorization</strong>.</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards"><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">C Backend</div><div class="card-body">NumPy\'s core is written in C and Fortran. Your Python code calls fast compiled routines under the hood -- no interpreter overhead per element.</div></div><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Vectorization</div><div class="card-body">Apply an operation to an entire array at once instead of looping element by element. One line of code replaces a for-loop.</div></div><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Contiguous Memory</div><div class="card-body">All elements sit side-by-side in memory. This enables CPU cache efficiency and SIMD instructions -- hardware-level parallelism.</div></div><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Fixed Types</div><div class="card-body">Every element has the same data type (e.g., float64). No type-checking per element means the CPU can blast through data.</div></div></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-compare"><div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-title">Python List</div><div class="compare-item">Each element is a full Python object (28+ bytes)</div><div class="compare-item">Type-checked at every operation</div><div class="compare-item">Elements scattered in memory</div><div class="compare-item">Must loop to apply operations</div><div class="compare-item">Flexible: can mix types [1, "hi", 3.14]</div></div><div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-title">NumPy Array</div><div class="compare-item">Each element is a raw number (4-8 bytes)</div><div class="compare-item">Single type -- no per-element checking</div><div class="compare-item">Contiguous memory block</div><div class="compare-item">Vectorized: one call processes all elements</div><div class="compare-item">Strict: all elements must be same type</div></div></div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n<span class="kw">import</span> time\n\nsize = <span class="num">1_000_000</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Python list approach</span>\npy_a = <span class="fn">list</span>(<span class="fn">range</span>(size))\npy_b = <span class="fn">list</span>(<span class="fn">range</span>(size))\n\nstart = time.<span class="fn">time</span>()\npy_c = [a + b <span class="kw">for</span> a, b <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">zip</span>(py_a, py_b)]\npy_time = time.<span class="fn">time</span>() - start\n\n<span class="cm"># NumPy approach</span>\nnp_a = np.<span class="fn">arange</span>(size)\nnp_b = np.<span class="fn">arange</span>(size)\n\nstart = time.<span class="fn">time</span>()\nnp_c = np_a + np_b   <span class="cm"># vectorized -- no loop!</span>\nnp_time = time.<span class="fn">time</span>() - start\n\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Python list: {py_time:.4f}s"</span>)  <span class="cm"># ~0.15s</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"NumPy array: {np_time:.4f}s"</span>) <span class="cm"># ~0.002s</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"NumPy is {py_time/np_time:.0f}x faster"</span>)</code></pre></div>'

/* --- Plotly: Speed Comparison EN --- */
+ '<div id="plot-speed-en" class="plotly-graph"></div>'
+ '<script>setTimeout(function(){'
+ 'var data=[{x:["Python List Loop","NumPy Vectorized"],y:[150,2],type:"bar",marker:{color:["#f87171","#4de87a"],line:{color:"rgba(255,255,255,0.2)",width:1}},text:["~150 ms","~2 ms"],textposition:"outside",textfont:{color:"#ebe6dc",size:13}}];'
+ 'var layout={title:{text:"Adding 1M Numbers: Python List vs NumPy",font:{color:"#ebe6dc",size:14}},paper_bgcolor:"rgba(0,0,0,0)",plot_bgcolor:"rgba(0,0,0,0)",font:{color:"#ebe6dc"},yaxis:{title:"Time (ms)",gridcolor:"rgba(255,255,255,0.06)",zerolinecolor:"rgba(255,255,255,0.15)"},xaxis:{gridcolor:"rgba(255,255,255,0.06)"},margin:{t:50,r:30,b:50,l:60},showlegend:false};'
