+ '<div class="calc-graph"><div class="graph-caption"><strong>What this graph shows:</strong> Adding one million numbers takes ~150 ms with a Python list comprehension but only ~2 ms with NumPy -- roughly 75x faster. The gap grows even larger for more complex operations like matrix multiplication.</div></div>'

+ '<div class="think-box"><div class="think-label">THINK ABOUT IT</div><div class="think-body">A neural network with 10 million parameters needs to compute gradients for every parameter at every training step. If each gradient computation took Python-loop time, training would take <em>weeks</em> instead of hours. This is why NumPy (and GPU libraries built on the same principle) matter.</div></div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 2: ndarray Fundamentals
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">2. ndarray Fundamentals</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>Core idea:</strong> The <code>ndarray</code> (n-dimensional array) is a <strong>grid of values, all of the same type</strong>, indexed by a tuple of non-negative integers. The number of dimensions is the <strong>rank</strong> of the array.</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text">Every ndarray has a set of attributes that describe its structure. Think of these as the array\'s "metadata" -- they tell you everything about the shape and memory layout without looking at the actual data.</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\n<span class="cm"># Create a 2D array (matrix)</span>\narr = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([[<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">3</span>],\n                [<span class="num">4</span>, <span class="num">5</span>, <span class="num">6</span>]])\n\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Array:\\n"</span>, arr)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"dtype   :"</span>, arr.dtype)      <span class="cm"># int64</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"shape   :"</span>, arr.shape)      <span class="cm"># (2, 3) -- 2 rows, 3 columns</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"ndim    :"</span>, arr.ndim)       <span class="cm"># 2 -- two dimensions</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"size    :"</span>, arr.size)       <span class="cm"># 6 -- total elements</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"itemsize:"</span>, arr.itemsize)   <span class="cm"># 8 -- bytes per element</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"nbytes  :"</span>, arr.nbytes)     <span class="cm"># 48 -- total memory (6 x 8)</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards"><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Data Type</div><div class="card-body">The type of every element: int64, float32, bool, etc. All elements share one type -- this is what makes NumPy fast.</div></div><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Shape</div><div class="card-body">A tuple giving the size of each dimension. (2, 3) means 2 rows and 3 columns. (4,) means a 1D array with 4 elements.</div></div><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Number of Dimensions</div><div class="card-body">How many axes the array has. 1D = vector, 2D = matrix, 3D = tensor. Equal to len(shape).</div></div><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Total Elements</div><div class="card-body">The product of all shape dimensions. A (2,3) array has 2 x 3 = 6 elements. A (3,4,5) array has 60.</div></div><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Bytes per Element</div><div class="card-body">How many bytes one element occupies. int64 = 8 bytes, float32 = 4 bytes, int8 = 1 byte.</div></div><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Total Memory</div><div class="card-body">Total bytes consumed: size x itemsize. A (1000, 1000) float64 array uses 8 MB.</div></div></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text">Understanding <strong>dimensionality terminology</strong> is essential for ML:</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-steps"><div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">0D</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Scalar</div><div class="step-detail"><code>np.array(42)</code> -- shape: (), ndim: 0 -- a single number wrapped in an array</div></div></div><div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">1D</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Vector</div><div class="step-detail"><code>np.array([1,2,3])</code> -- shape: (3,), ndim: 1 -- feature vector, word embedding, signal</div></div></div><div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">2D</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Matrix</div><div class="step-detail"><code>np.array([[1,2],[3,4]])</code> -- shape: (2,2), ndim: 2 -- dataset, grayscale image, weight matrix</div></div></div><div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">3D</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">3D Tensor</div><div class="step-detail"><code>shape: (batch, rows, cols)</code> -- batch of sequences, RGB image, time series batch</div></div></div><div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">4D</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">4D Tensor</div><div class="step-detail"><code>shape: (batch, height, width, channels)</code> -- batch of color images in CNNs</div></div></div></div>'

/* --- Plotly: Memory Comparison EN --- */
+ '<div id="plot-memory-en" class="plotly-graph"></div>'
